  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn upper level disturbance will move through this afternoon and kick \noff isolated showers. Instability is expected to be limited with \nMLCAPE values less than 500 J/kg though 0-6 km bulk shear will be 50 \nto 60 kts, so a few thunderstorms will also be possible. We may have \nto monitor how the environment evolves this afternoon because if \ninstability increases higher than modeled, then there would be \npotential for strong, pulse-like convection. The best chances will \nbe east of I-81 in central NY.\n\nSurface high pressure builds into the region later today. This high \nwill remain in place through Saturday, extending the period of dry \nand pleasant conditions for most. Tonight, skies will become partly \nto mostly clear and winds will be calm so fog is expected to develop.\nTemperatures will fall into the 40s and 50s overnight before \nbouncing back into the 70s and 80s on Saturday. Saturday night will \nhave similar conditions as tonight, though may be a touch warmer for \nsome as cloud cover increases ahead of the next system. There is a \nlow chance for showers over far western portions of the region, \nthough most guidance holds off until Sunday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe ridge of high pressure will break down on Sunday. A surface low \nwill deepen slightly as it moves across the lower Great Lakes \nregion. Some showers ahead of this system will be possible by Sunday \nafternoon, though the bulk of the precipitation will be later in the \nday through Sunday night. PWATs will increase to 1.75 to 2 inches, \nand there will warm cloud depths of at least 10,000 feet. These \nconditions will favor heavy rainfall. The one plus will be this \nsystem is expected to move along fairly quickly and the potential \nfor training showers is low. Despite this, models are showing \nlocalized precipitation accumulations of 1 to 3 inches with most \nguidance favoring the bulk of rain over northeast PA. This lines up \nwell with WPC's thinking and the ERO Slight Risk clipping the \nWyoming Valley. There does remain uncertainty on where the heaviest \nrainfall will indeed fall and how much there will be. This drier \nstretch has allowed soil conditions to dry out some, so urban and \npoor drainage areas are where localized hydro issues will be most \nlikely. Models have backed off on on instability over the region, so \nstronger storms are not expected. NBM guidance maintains low chances \nfor thunder, which if showers move in later, that would allow for \nsome instability to develop. Lingering moisture behind the system \nwill support additional rain showers on Monday. PWATs will \nsignificantly decrease by then, so these showers will be light.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nHigh pressure builds into the region on Tuesday, though some models \nkeep the lingering showers around into the morning. Otherwise, \nconditions will be dry through a period of time with a slight \nwarming trend. The next system with widespread rain chances will \nthen move in by Thursday, though uncertainties remain. Guidance like \nthe GFS have an open wave moving through while the ECMWF has a \nclosed low. There are timing differences as well with the GFS being \nmuch quicker than most other guidance.",
